# Line endings are pinned per script type instead of being left to each clone's
# core.autocrlf. Both failure modes are real and have bitten this repo:
#   - a .bat checked out with LF makes cmd's `goto`/`call :label` scanner miss
#     labels that are plainly in the file ("cannot find the batch label").
#   - a .sh checked out with CRLF makes the kernel read the shebang as
#     "/usr/bin/env bash\r" and fail with "bad interpreter".
# On a Windows clone with autocrlf=true, git wants to hand build.sh CRLF, which
# is the second case. These rules make the checkout correct either way.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
